Sprite examples
===============

Each subdirectory holds one example.  An example has a README, a run script,
and a file expected.out with the exact output of the run script.  Most
examples have a Python driver, go.py, and one Curry module.  Run "make stage"
from the root of the repository first; the run scripts use the staged
install under ../install, or the install named by SPRITE_HOME.  To run an
example, run its script:

    ./run

A run script uses the installed Sprite with the flags of
SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS.  The script of 03-inspect adds interpret:off to
them, so the module is compiled before it runs and the example prints the
first line of the generated C++ (its README says why).  The script of
04-static-compile selects the reference backend of the developer notes of
the documentation, because that example runs on it only.

The first run of an example also compiles its Curry module, which takes
about a second per module.  The build products go to .curry/ next to the
source.  The Curry front end warns on a function whose rules overlap, and
Sprite reports the warning on the standard error stream when it compiles
the module.  In several examples the rules overlap by design: ndinsert in
Queens builds a permutation by a choice.  The run scripts of those examples
set SPRITE_FRONTEND_WARNINGS=0 unless the environment sets the variable,
so a first run prints no warning; set it to 1 to see them.  A README
states what the example shows about functional logic programming, what it
shows about calling Curry from Python, how to run it, the output to expect,
and the sizes that keep the run short.

The test tests/unit_examples.py runs every run script in a child process and
compares its output with expected.out.  It runs 03-inspect, 11-sudoku,
12-cryptarithm and 22-cxx-types-trait-check on the C++ backend only, and
04-static-compile on the reference backend only.  To regenerate the expected
output after a deliberate change, run SPRITE_UPDATE_EXPECTED=1 ./run_tests
unit_examples.py from tests/, and review the diff.

The list below gives each directory, the title of its README, and what it
teaches.  The numbers group the examples: 00 to 04 tour the API, 10 to 19
and 24 are programs with a Python driver, and 20 to 23 are a series over one
shared library.  The gaps are deliberate.

00-run-curry-programs
    Run Curry programs with sprite-exec.  Call-time choice; a program by
    file, by module, and by goal.

01-run-with-python
    Run a Curry program from Python.  Import a module and iterate over the
    values of a goal.

02-dynamic-code-generation
    Compile Curry code at run time.  curry.compile for a module and for an
    expression; curry.expr.

03-inspect
    Inspect Curry objects from Python.  curry.inspect: symbols, types,
    ICurry files, generated code.

04-static-compile
    Save a compiled Curry module.  curry.save with a goal, and running the
    saved script.

10-queens-set-functions
    N queens with set functions.  Non-determinism as values, functional
    patterns, set functions.

11-sudoku
    Sudoku by search.  Search as the program; the most constrained cell
    first; a dead branch dies early.

12-cryptarithm
    Cryptarithms: SEND + MORE = MONEY.  Structural permutations and
    laziness as pruning.

13-regex-nondeterminism
    Regular expressions by non-determinism.  A semantics as a function;
    the words of a pattern as values; matching by unification; free
    variables.

14-parser-functional-patterns
    An expression parser from functional patterns.  Functional patterns
    split the input; failure prunes parses.

15-dependency-solver
    A package dependency solver.  Resolution as a search over Python data;
    the preference as the order of the candidates; the preferred plan
    pinned by a set function; a failure explained; a lockfile.

16-scheduling
    Scheduling tasks fed from Python data.  Constraints as guards;
    optimization by failure; guard order.

17-type-inference
    Type inference by unification.  Free variables as unknown types; =:= as
    unification.

18-text-extraction
    Text extraction with functional patterns, compiled at run time.  Rules
    as Curry text; Curry extracts, Python aggregates.

19-blocks-world-app
    Blocks World as a command-line application.  Search by iterative
    deepening; argparse; an http.server form.

20-cxx-types-deduction
    Template argument deduction over a model of C++ types.  Free variables
    as template parameters; deduction by unification; set functions; a
    bounded generator for the inverse question.

21-cxx-types-overloads
    Overload resolution over a model of C++ types.  Conversions as
    non-deterministic rules; the viable set as a set function; the inverse
    question with a free variable.

22-cxx-types-trait-check
    Trait checking by narrowing over a model of C++ types.  A bounded
    generator; narrowing with =:=; set functions collect counterexamples.

23-cxx-types-layout
    Member layout by search over a model of C++ types.  Non-determinism as
    member orders; set functions; a constraint as a guard; a reflection-like
    Python description.

24-build-system
    A makefile in Curry.  Variables as definitions with the names of make;
    a rule as an equation of a relation with the inputs in the head; the
    pattern rule as a functional pattern; a condition as an if or a guard,
    reached from the command line; the header edges as a relation; the
    plan, the ready set and the inverse query from the relations; three
    lints; a driver that runs the recipes.

Other files in this directory
-----------------------------

cxx/ holds the Curry modules that the examples 20 to 23 share: the
algebraic model of C++ types with its traits, its printer and its
generator (CxxType), the fundamental types and the tables of the LP64 data
model (CxxLimits), overload resolution (CxxOverload), the trait check
(CxxCheck) and the member layout (CxxLayout).  The directory has no run
script, so the test does not run it.  The README of 20-cxx-types-deduction
introduces the series.

Peano.curry is the program of the Quickstart in the user guide.  Run it
with sprite-exec from this directory.

05-blocks-in-flask is an older Flask application.  It needs the Flask
package, which is not part of the staged install, and a slow solver.
19-blocks-world-app replaces it, and the test does not run it.
